</section>
<section id="A1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.4 </span>Analysis of Task Generalization</h3>

<div id="A1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PPE is inherently task-agnostic and can be seamlessly integrated into any clustering-based token merging method, regardless of downstream task. While our main experiments focus on MLLM QA tasks—largely due to their popularity and the convenience of evaluation—we emphasize that PPE is not limited to QA scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further demonstrate its generalization capability, we conducted additional experiments on the image captioning task using Flickr30k <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Young et al. (2014)</a></cite> benchmark. We reused the same models from Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.2 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ Positional Preservation Embedding for Multimodal Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and directly evaluated their zero-shot captioning performance without any further fine-tuning. The results are in Table <a href="#A1.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ A.4 Analysis of Task Generalization ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Positional Preservation Embedding for Multimodal Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T9" class="ltx_table">
<div id="A1.T9.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:41.6pt;vertical-align:-18.7pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-36.7pt,3.8pt) scale(0.844103666560858,0.844103666560858) ;">
